# Age of Wonders (1) — binary modding project

This directory is the installed AoW1 game. Mods are applied as **binary patches** to the game's
Delphi 3 modules (`AoWEPACK.dpl`, `AoWTCPCK.dpl`, `aowInt.dpl`) and executables (`AoW.exe`,
`AoWCompat.exe`) — no source, no new DLLs. All reverse-engineering notes, patch scripts, and the RE
toolkit live in **`Modding Resources/`**.

## Recording convention — IMPORTANT

**Record at APPLY time, not at confirmation time.** The moment a patch is written to a live binary,
write its doc and its index row with the status `🔨 APPLIED, UNTESTED (date)`. Do not wait for the
user's in-game test to start documenting — waiting is how a session ends with six applied features
and nothing on disk explaining them, forcing the next session to re-derive everything from the
build scripts.

### The status ladder — every feature doc and index row carries exactly one

| status | means | who sets it |
|---|---|---|
| `SPECULATIVE` | analysed / designed, **not** applied to any binary | anyone |
| `🔨 BUILT, NOT APPLIED (date)` | a build script exists and dry-runs clean; nothing written | anyone |
| `🔨 APPLIED, UNTESTED (date)` | written to a live binary; static checks pass; **nobody has played it** | anyone |
| `✅ CONFIRMED WORKING (date)` | the user tested it in-game and it works | **only the user's test** |
| `🛑 REVERTED (date, why)` | applied then backed out — keep the RE, say why | anyone |

**Never label something confirmed on your own say-so.** Static verification, byte-checks, QA passes
and clean round-trips justify `APPLIED, UNTESTED` and nothing more — a cave can verify perfectly and
still be wrong, and this project has repeatedly proved it (a cave that ran at package init passed
every static check and broke startup; a PIC anchor `0x4C` short assembled, verified, and read the
wrong memory forever).

### What an `APPLIED, UNTESTED` record must contain

Enough for a future session to pick the work up cold, **and** to undo it:

- addresses, cave VAs + byte counts, struct/field offsets, the *why*;
- the **exact revert command** (the script's surgical `--undo`), and the revert **order** where
  patches share a cave or a hook;
- **cross-feature couplings** — "undoing X silently re-breaks Y" is the single most expensive thing
  to rediscover, and it will not be obvious from either script alone;
- failed approaches with the reason they failed, so they aren't retried;
- the **in-game checklist the user still needs to run** — this is what turns the row green later.

- **Update the relevant master index** (currently `Modding Resources/Zig notes/Unit_Spellcasting_INDEX.md`) —
  status, which build script, which binary + backup name.

**On the user's confirmation**, upgrade the status to `✅ CONFIRMED WORKING (date)` and drop the
in-game checklist. That is usually the whole edit.

The point: a future session must be able to pick up the work from the docs without re-deriving.
Unconfirmed or speculative findings are recorded too — clearly flagged, never silently omitted.

### Superseded analysis — delete it unless it might be useful

When a finding turns out to be wrong, the **default is to delete the wrong text** and put the correct
finding in its place (or a one-line pointer to the doc that now holds it). Don't leave `SUPERSEDED` /
`RETRACTED` banners, strikethrough, or "the verdict below is wrong, here's why" preambles sitting next
to the old reasoning — a wrong analysis left in the record costs every future session the time to read
it *and* re-adjudicate it, and risks it being acted on.

**Retain a corrected or superseded explanation only if it might actually be useful later.** That's a
judgement call. Things that usually earn their place:

- a **failed approach** — something tried that didn't work, with *why* (so it isn't retried);
- a wrong theory **tempting enough that it would plausibly be re-derived** — flag it "don't re-try
  as-is" and give the reason it fails;
- **reusable machinery** found along the way (addresses, call paths, struct offsets) that is still
  valid even though the conclusion built on it was wrong.

When it stays, compress it to the useful part — a bullet or a short section, not the original
write-up — and say plainly that it's superseded and by what. When it doesn't, delete it outright.

Either way, fix invalidated claims in the master indexes and other docs **at the source** rather than
appending a contradiction.

## How patches are built

- **One Python build script per feature** in `Modding Resources/build_scripts/` (`build_*.py`;
  runnable from anywhere: `python build_scripts/build_X.py`).
  Model:
  keystone-assembled caves, capstone-disassembled for review, **dry-run by default / `--apply` to
  write, idempotent, verify-before-write (aborts on byte mismatch), auto-backup** to
  `<file>.pre-<feature>`. Re-running with no args verifies current state.
- **Revert** = the build script's surgical `--undo`. Restoring a `.pre-*` backup is the fallback, and
  usually the wrong move — see below. Note the revert order in a feature's doc when patches share a
  code cave / PE section.

### ⚠ A `.pre-*` snapshot is not a revert path — assume you cannot revert by copying

**On 2026-08-08 roughly 100 `.pre-*` snapshots were deleted deliberately** (each is a full 2.7 MB
copy; ~270 MB of bloat), and `Modding Resources/backups/` **no longer exists** — do not go looking
for it, and disregard any doc that says features live in two backup directories.

⚠ **The root-level snapshots have since re-accumulated, and will keep doing so** — every `--apply`
mints one by convention. Measured 2026-09-02: **81 files, 178 MB** (55 AoWEPACK.dpl, 9 AoWTCPCK.dpl,
5 each AoW.exe/AoWCompat.exe, 3 aowInt.dpl, 2 each AoWEd.exe/vcl30.dpl), mtimes 2025-08-26 →
2026-09-02, 67 of them from the 2026-08 feature run. **Never quote a count from this file — derive
it:**

```bash
ls -la --time-style=+"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M" *.pre-*     # the real inventory, root only
```

A long list is *not* a safety net: **every feature's revert path is its script's `--undo`, not a
file.** Surgical undo is what the convention already preferred, it survives both layering and
pruning, and a `.pre-*` restore still wipes every layer applied to that file afterwards.
`re_tools/bisect_dll.py` binary-searches this stack to find which feature broke something — it has
material to work with again, but only for files whose snapshots were taken from a *proven* baseline
(see the byte-check warning below).

**Two pristine references are load-bearing. Do not delete these in the next cleanup:**

| file | why it must survive |
|---|---|
| `Modding Resources/AoWEPACK_original_backup.dpl` | the pristine pre-modding DLL — the "is this vanilla behaviour?" byte-diff reference |
| `Ziggurat upload/AoW.exe` (2025-03-21) | the **only** unpatched exe left; `build_herodlg_columns.py --apply` copies the vanilla fill loop out of it and cannot rebuild without it |

⚠ **A `.pre-<feature>` file is not proof of anything — byte-check it.** `AoW.exe.pre-herodlgcolumns`
was written by a rebuild that ran *after* the feature was installed, so it is a snapshot of the
previous **patched** build, not a pristine one. Verify a reference against known-vanilla bytes
before trusting it; never go by the filename.

⚠ **A `.pre-<feature>` restore still wipes every layer applied to that file afterwards** — that part
has not changed, it just matters less now that so few exist. `shutil.copy2` preserves mtimes, so
sorting gives the true layer order. Only the newest backup for a file is a safe one-layer revert,
and "newest" expires silently the moment anyone patches that file again — derive it, never quote a
count from a doc.

**So: never write "revert and re-apply" as a re-tune procedure.** Instead make the build script
**rewrite its caves in place** — verify-before-write against *either* the currently-installed bytes *or*
the new ones, overwrite, and assert that any zone the cave grows into is still zero. Take a fresh
feature-named backup so the revert path stays one layer deep.
`build_scripts/build_invis_penalty.py` is the worked example (it rewrites caves owned by
`build_trueseeing.py` without reverting `.pre-trueseeing`, which by then had five features stacked on
it — and `.pre-trueseeing` has since been deleted outright, which is the stronger argument: the
in-place rewrite still works, a revert-based procedure would now be impossible).

**Better still: give the script a surgical `--undo`** that restores the hook/VMT slot and zeroes its own
cave, verify-before-write, touching no backup. That undo path survives both layering and pruning.
Worked examples: `build_sitedefender_vary.py`, `build_magebane.py`, `build_mastery_cost.py`,
`build_stormeffectroll.py`, `build_crusade_spawns.py` (`--undo`); `build_ai_itemtarget.py`,
`build_debuffcache.py`, `build_effectroll.py`, `build_hpbar_clamp.py` (`--revert`). ⚠ Not every
`--revert` flag is surgical — `build_mapcursor_fix.py --revert` just restores `.pre-mapcursor`, so it is
exactly as destructive as the manual copy. Read the flag's implementation before trusting it.
- **Caves must be position-independent** (rel32 / register-only, no absolute mem refs): the `.dpl`
  packages rebase at runtime. Reuse a VMT slot that already has a `.reloc` entry, or jump *into*
  existing code to borrow its already-relocated globals. Exe caves (fixed base 0x400000) may use
  absolute addresses.
- **Game files are locked while any AoW binary is running** (`AoW.exe`, `AoWCompat.exe`,
  `AoWDevEd.exe`, `AoWEd.exe`). A lock shows as "Device or resource busy" / PermissionError. The
  editor `AoWDevEd.exe` loads `AoWEPACK.dpl`, so it locks the DLL too.

  **⚠ Just kill them — standing authorization, don't ask.** If a lock is blocking an `--apply`,
  `--undo`, or a file write, terminate the process yourself and retry; do NOT stop and ask the user
  to close the game. There is nothing to lose: the game autosaves per turn and the editor prompts on
  its own next launch. Asking instead of killing has stalled real work mid-session.

  ```powershell
  Get-Process | Where-Object { $_.ProcessName -match '^(AoW|AoWCompat|AoWDevEd|AoWEd)$' } | Stop-Process -Force
  ```

  (`AowEmailWrapper` and `Launcher` do **not** lock the binaries — leave them alone. Match the
  process names exactly with `^...$`, or the `-match` will also hit `AowEmailWrapper`.)
- **Per-executable patches only affect that binary.** `AoW.exe` (the game) and `AoWDevEd.exe`
  (the editor) are different builds and often call *different* functions for the same feature —
  verify the actual call path per binary; don't assume they share code.
- **`AoWCompat.exe` is NOT a separate binary to analyze** (verified by byte-diff 2026-07-24): it is
  `AoW.exe` with **exactly one byte changed** — file offset `0x3BB7C` (VA `0x43C77C`), the reported
  build number in the MP version check, `0x0F` (v1.36.0015) → `0x05` (v1.36.0005). It exists only
  for multiplayer against 1.36.0005 peers. Every AoW.exe address, finding, and patch applies to it
  verbatim; keep patching both in lockstep, never reverse-engineer it separately. If it is ever
  lost: copy `AoW.exe` and flip that one byte.

## A cave that rolls must draw from the RIGHT generator — IMPORTANT

AoW1 has **two** random generators, and picking the wrong one is silent: it assembles, it
verifies, it plays perfectly in single player, and the peers paint different maps.

| | address | draws from |
|---|---|---|
| **SYNCED** | `AoWE.TAoWHSMap.Random @0x5577827C` (EAX=map, EDX=n → 0..n-1) | `map[+0x230]`, replicated, written back — also the value the out-of-sync comparator reads |
| **RAW** | `System.@RandInt` via AoWEPACK thunk `0x55701080` (EAX=n) | `System.RandSeed`, a **per-process** global |

**The rule: a cave draws from the generator the function it is injected into already uses.**
Where that function makes no draw of its own, the roll's *subject* decides — replicated game
state (terrain, spawns, rosters, items, damage) ⇒ SYNCED; pixels ⇒ RAW.

Never guess it, and never reason it out from first principles — vanilla already answered, and
one command prints the answer for every function in the pristine DLL:

```bash
python "Modding Resources/re_tools/rng_audit.py" --functions
```

⚠ **Inside tactical combat the correct answer is RAW and SYNCED is wrong.** `TCombat.Execute
@0x557282C8` is a one-liner that does `System.RandSeed := TAoWHSMap.Random(map,$FFFFFF)`, so a
battle re-anchors the raw seed from one synchronised draw and then runs deterministically off
raw draws. A synced draw mid-combat trips the `GetSynchronised` guard *and* perturbs the sync
value. That same one-line re-anchor is the sanctioned bridge when a cave in a synchronised
context must call an engine routine that draws internally (`build_arena.py` before `GenerateItem`).

⚠ **Setting bit 3 of `[*0x558FA040 + 0x3C]` does not make a synced call safe — it makes it
raw.** That flag makes `TAoWHSMap.Random` skip its seed bookkeeping entirely. It is the known
way to silence the "Invalid AoWHSMap.Random use" modal, and silencing that diagnostic is all it
does. If the popup fires, the context is not synchronised: the hook site is wrong, or the roll
belongs further up in the TE body.

**After `--apply`, re-run `rng_audit.py --owners` and confirm the new site prints `ok`.** The
tool diffs every module against its pristine reference and catches call/jmp rel32, indirect
calls, *and* bare address constants in every EXECUTE section (a cave can reach the RNG through
the rebase-delta idiom with no call target to match, and can live outside `CODE`/`.text`).

Full derivation, the vanilla SYNC/RAW function split, the canonical call preamble, and the audit
of all 25 modded sites: **`Modding Resources/Zig notes/RNG_Lockstep_Rule.md`**.

## No machine-specific paths — IMPORTANT

**Never hard-code an absolute path containing the user's profile directory** (`C:\Users\<name>\...`)
in anything under `Modding Resources/` — scripts *or* docs. These files get shared with other modders,
and a user-profile path leaks the real name. Resolve the game directory **relative to the script's own
location** instead; `build_scripts/` and `re_tools/` both sit exactly two levels below it:

```python
GAME = os.environ.get("AOW_GAME_DIR") or os.path.abspath(
    os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "..", ".."))
```

(`import os` must precede it. In `re_tools/`, also use
`TOOLS = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))`.) This keeps "runs from any working directory"
while working unchanged on someone else's install; `AOW_GAME_DIR` overrides for a different install
or if the script is moved out of the folder.

In **docs**, write `<game dir>\Save\foo.hsm`, not a real absolute path. Same for any other personal
data — no emails, logins, or machine names. Applied retroactively 2026-07-20: 46 scripts + 3 docs were
de-personalised; verify with `grep -rlI "<profile name>" "Modding Resources/"` before sharing.

### ⚠ A resolved path must never be BAKED INTO A PATCHED BINARY — derive it at runtime

The rule above governs **source text**. On its own it is not enough, because a build script can follow
it perfectly and still leak: resolve `GAME` relative to the script (clean source), then write that
*resolved* absolute string into the cave blob it patches into a binary. The name moves from the place
the convention watches to the place it cannot see.

**This has happened.** `build_dlgdirs.py` did exactly that — `INI_PATH = GAME + r"\AoWEd_LastDirs.ini"`
encoded into the blob — putting the profile path inside `HSEPack.dpl` and `AoWDevEd.exe`, undetected
because both live *outside* `Modding Resources/` and are binaries a folder grep skips. A third-party
modder hit the identical defect in his own copy and diagnosed it before we did
(`Modding Resources/Inioch/Inioch_Share4_Share5_Catalogue.md`, trap #17).

**So: if a cave needs a path, derive it at runtime.** `GetModuleFileNameA(NULL, buf, MAX_PATH)`, scan
back to the last `\`, append the leaf filename. Never embed an absolute path built at *build* time.
⭐ Check the module's **own import table first** — both editor binaries already import
`GetModuleFileNameA` from kernel32 with a ready-made Delphi thunk, so it is one `call rel32` to an
existing thunk, with no VCL30-delta hop and no import-table surgery.

This is a **correctness** rule as much as a privacy one: a baked path makes the binary work on exactly
one install, and it fails *silently* — Win32 profile APIs resolve a bare filename against `%WINDIR%`,
so reads come back empty and writes vanish with no error.

### The check — two scans, because one folder grep sees neither problem

```bash
U="$USERNAME"                                # never write the name into the command itself
grep -laF  "$U" *.exe *.dpl                  # binaries in the game root
grep -rlaF "$U" "Modding Resources/"         # the shared folder -- NOTE -a, NEVER -I
```

Stronger still for a cave, and name-independent: assert that **no drive-letter absolute path of any
kind** (`[A-Za-z]:\`) appears anywhere within the section extent, and that a bare leaf filename occurs
exactly once, preceded by a NUL. A leaf filename in a cave is correct and is not a leak.

⚠ **Use `-F`, or ripgrep.** The escaped-regex forms — `grep -rn "C:\\Users"` and `grep -rn 'C:\Users'`
— **silently return zero matches here** while `-F` finds 17 files. A check that reports clean because
of a quoting bug is worse than no check.

⚠ A `.pre-*` snapshot taken *after* the leaking patch carries the leak too. Scan those as well, or
delete them.

⚠⚠ **`-I` is the trap. Use `-a`.** `-I` skips "binary" files and hid **59 of 66** hits when this was
first checked — the folder looked like 7 text files and was actually 66. Three sources, none of them
hand-written:

| source | count | why it leaks |
|---|---|---|
| `re_tools/__pycache__/*.pyc` | 49 | Python embeds the **absolute source path** in every `.pyc`. ⚠ Deleting these is **not durable — they come back the moment anyone runs a script.** Either export `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, or treat `find "Modding Resources" -name '*.pyc' -delete` as a mandatory **pre-share step**, run after the last script run and immediately before packaging. |
| `AoW1 Modding/*.rep`, `.lock`, `.gbf`, `.prp` | 16 | the Ghidra project records the owner username and absolute paths in its internal DB. |
| `Ziggurat Manual.log`, one catalogue doc | 2 | ordinary text. |

The lesson generalises: **the leak is usually in something a tool generated, not in something anyone
typed** — bytecode caches, project databases, logs, and patched binaries. Those are exactly the files
a "source is clean" review never opens.

⚠ **`Modding Resources/AoW1 Modding/` must never be included when the folder is shared.** That is the
Ghidra project, and its internal DB (`.rep/`, `.lock`, `.gbf`, `.prp`) records the owner's username and
absolute paths in a form that cannot be scrubbed without risking the project. It is working state, not
a deliverable — exclude the directory rather than trying to clean it. Ruled 2026-08-31; the other three
leak sources were cleaned that day (49 `.pyc` deleted, 4 `.pre-*` snapshots deleted, 2 text files
de-personalised), leaving this directory as the only remaining source in the tree.

## No inline explanations in generated GUIs — IMPORTANT

Anything user-facing this project generates (the Ziggurat Manual, contact sheets, editor UIs)
carries **controls and data only**. No explanatory prose, no legends, no "how to use this"
sentences, no "this table shows..." intros, no capability advertisements. If an interaction
isn't discoverable, fix the control — label it, resize it, move it to the top — rather than
captioning it. The user will ask for a label when one is wanted.

## The live game is on a 5% / doubled-stat scale — Ghidra shows the 10% vanilla one — IMPORTANT

**Vanilla AoW1 runs on 10-percentage-point increments per point of ATK/DEF/RES. Ziggurat halved every
to-hit slope to 5pp and doubled every ATK/DEF/RES source**, so hit chances and damage land where they
did in vanilla and only half-steps are new. `build_scripts/build_hitslope5.py` owns the conversion
(**7** slope sites — not 8); see `Zig notes/HitChance_Increment_Audit.md` and
`FivePct_Conversion_Manifest.md`, plus `DamHP_Double_Decisions.md` for the separate DAM/HP doubling.

⚠ **This is the easiest thing in the project to get wrong, because Ghidra's image is the PRISTINE
VANILLA DLL** (see the RE toolkit section). Every decompile shows the *vanilla* slope. Quoting one
yields numbers wrong by a factor of two that look entirely reasonable, and nothing flags it.

Worked example, found 2026-09-01: `AoWE.ExecuteDamageRole @0x55725EAC` decompiles as
`t = 10 - 2*diff`. The live DLL is `t = 10 - diff` — vanilla `8b c6 03 c0`
(`mov eax,esi; add eax,eax`) is live `8b c6 90 90`, the doubling **nop-ed out**. So a point of stat
difference is worth 5pp, not 10, and that roll's usable band is ±8 stat points, not ±4. An analysis
built on the decompiled form had to be redone.

**The rule: never quote a slope, increment, or clamp from a decompile — byte-diff it first.**

```bash
python "Modding Resources/re_tools/dasm.py" AoWEPACK.dpl <VA> <len>
# then compare against Modding Resources/AoWEPACK_original_backup.dpl for the vanilla bytes
```

⚠ **Do not assume a given number WAS converted.** The doubling was applied source-by-source and has
known gaps — e.g. Turn Undead's `GetTouchAttack` still returns the vanilla 9/10/11/12, so against
doubled RES it is floored against any undead with RES ≥ 20. Wherever a **stat** is compared against a
**constant**, verify that both moved; a half-converted comparison is silent.

⚠ **A roll designed for 1–10 stats saturates fast on doubled ones.** Ranges that were comfortable in
vanilla (±4, ±8) are now crossed by a single strong stat, pinning results at their 10 %/90 % clamps
and quietly flattening whatever else was meant to matter there (ability level, difficulty). Check
where a formula saturates before concluding it scales.

## Numbers the user proposes are ALREADY in the current scale — IMPORTANT

When the user specifies a game value — heal amount, damage, HP, cost, stat bonus, duration — **it is
already expressed in the current Ziggurat scale.** Take it at face value and build it verbatim.

**Never ask which scale was meant.** Not "did you mean +5, or +5 in vanilla terms (= +10 after the
DAM/HP doubling)?", and never silently rescale it to be helpful. The user designed the rebalance; the
doubling is not news to them, and reflecting it back at them is noise. Ruled 2026-08-31 — their words:
*"I am always going to propose numbers appropriate to the new scale and don't need to be reminded."*

If a value still looks wrong for the scale, that is a **finding to state in one line while building it
as specified** — not a question that blocks the work. This forbids only the "which scale did you mean"
class; genuine design questions (does it apply in auto-resolve, can heroes take it, does it stack)
remain worth asking.

**Applies to `aow-pm`, `aow-coder` and `aow-qa` too** — a spec must not carry a rescale question back
to the user, and QA must not flag a literal value as a scale defect.

## ⚠ The .xlsx workbooks are HISTORICAL — outdated, not a source of truth

`Modding Resources/modding spreadsheets/*.xlsx` (`Ziggurat Engine Notes.xlsx`,
`AoW1 Hex Editing Notes.xlsx`) record the design as it stood **before** the 2026-08 rebalance
passes. They have **not** been maintained since and now disagree with the shipped game in places.
Confirmed stale examples on the `misc` sheet (2026-08-27): row 41 `To-hit increment per ATK/DEF/RES`
still reads 0.1 for both columns although Ziggurat halved it to 0.05; row 35 `Max Hero stats with
buffs` reads 20 although the hero clamps are 40; row 40 `Max hero HP` reads 80 although the cap is
120. Assume there are more.

**Rules:**

- **Never cite the workbook as evidence** of what the game currently does, and never "verify" a
  finding against it. The live binaries and `Release/*.pfs` are the truth; the feature docs in
  `Modding Resources/Zig notes/` are the record of intent.
- **Do not edit the workbooks to bring them up to date** (user ruling 2026-08-27). They are kept as
  a historical snapshot of the original design pass. Editing them would destroy that.
- `build_ziggurat_manual.py` still **reads** the workbook for the vanilla-vs-Ziggurat comparison
  columns and the General Rules rows, so some manual tables carry the stale numbers. That is known
  and accepted. **New manual content is hand-written in the builder or derived live from `.pfs` /
  the DLL — never added to the workbook.** Where a hand-written section and a workbook-derived table
  disagree, the hand-written one is right; say so when it comes up rather than silently reconciling.

## RE toolkit & scope

- `Modding Resources/re_tools/` — PE/VMT/DFM parsers, IAT-ref finder (`pescan.py`), annotated exe
  disassembler (`dump_exe.py`). Needs `pip install capstone keystone-engine`.
- **Ghidra (MCP) has `AoWEPACK.dpl` only.** For `AoW.exe` / `AoWTCPCK.dpl` / `aowInt.dpl` /
  `AoWDevEd.exe`, use `re_tools/` (capstone). Verify which module actually calls a function before
  hooking — a symbol being *exported* by the DLL is not proof the exe *calls* it (check `iatrefs`).
  **Setup + startup + rollback: `Modding Resources/Zig notes/Ghidra_Toolchain.md`.**
  ⭐ **The DLL exports its own symbol table — 10,036 names in `.edata`** (it is a Delphi 3 *package*),
  so a VMT slot can be named straight from the file with no Ghidra at all. 18 classes / 1394 slots
  already derived in `Modding Resources/Zig notes/Ghidra_VMT_Layouts.md`; ⚠ `Destroy` lives at **`VMT-0x04`**.
  **Field offsets: `Modding Resources/Zig notes/Ghidra_Field_Catalogue.md` is canonical** — class-keyed,
  bounds-checked against measured instance sizes, generated by `re_tools/ghidra_fields.py`.
  ⚠ **An offset means nothing without its class** (five aliasing traps found 2026-08-03). The Ghidra
  image carries structs, enums and a `[LIVE-PATCH]` comment at every site where the live DLL differs;
  `Modding Resources/Zig notes/Ghidra_Annotations.md` explains all of it and rebuilds it in three commands. Since 2026-08-03 this is
  Ghidra **12.1.2** + GhidraMCP **6.0.0** (267 tools, **port 8089**), project
  `Modding Resources\AoW1 Modding\AoW1-vanilla.gpr`, program `AoWEPACK_vanilla.dpl`. The old
  Ghidra 11.3.2 + GhidraMCP 1.4 (27 tools, port 8080) is still installed as the rollback path.

  **⚠ If the Ghidra MCP is down, NAG THE USER TO RESTART IT — don't just fall back and carry on.**
  The symptom is `Max retries exceeded` / `WinError 10061 target machine actively refused it` on
  `127.0.0.1:8089` from any `mcp__ghidra__*` call. Say so **immediately and explicitly**, and ask
  them to restart Ghidra + the MCP bridge; then keep working with `re_tools/` in the meantime and
  ask again at the next natural pause if it is still down. Diagnose from outside Claude first —
  `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8089/check_connection` says whether it is Ghidra or the bridge that is
  down.

  Why this is a rule and not a nicety: capstone gives you *bytes*, Ghidra gives you *xrefs,
  decompiles and types*. Silently degrading to capstone turns a five-minute "who calls this / what
  is this field" into an hour of hand-disassembly, and it biases the work toward whatever is easy
  to read linearly. A whole session was spent this way on 2026-07-30/31 before anyone noticed the
  MCP had simply not been started.

  (Ghidra's image is the **pristine vanilla** `AoWEPACK.dpl`, re-verified 2026-08-03 — a good vanilla
  reference, but it carries **none** of this project's ~518 patched byte-runs. Byte-check the live
  file before concluding anything is unpatched. **Keeping it vanilla is deliberate — don't propose
  importing the live DLL:** caves are PIC via `call $+5; pop; sub`, which wrecks Ghidra's stack
  analysis, and `E9` hooks truncate the host function, so patched code decompiles *worse* live than
  vanilla. Read patched regions with `re_tools/dasm.py AoWEPACK.dpl <VA> <len>` instead.
  See the Investigation-traps memory.)

  ⚠⚠ **The `[LIVE-PATCH]` comment layer GOES STALE, and its absence is silent.** It is what makes the
  vanilla image safe to read, so it must be re-pushed after every applied feature:

  ```bash
  python "Modding Resources/re_tools/ghidra_annots.py"            # dry run: inventory
  python "Modding Resources/re_tools/ghidra_annots.py" --apply    # push the comments
  ```

  Measured 2026-09-01: sites owned by `build_combatlog_dll.py`, `build_invis_penalty.py`,
  `build_turnundead_res.py` and `build_touchlog_gate.py` carried comments, while **all four
  `build_hitslope5.py` sites had none** (`0x55725D9D` — inside `HitRole` itself — plus `0x55725DCF`,
  `0x55725E3B`, `0x55725ED7`). The comments predate that feature, so **the whole 5 % conversion was
  invisible in Ghidra** — which is exactly how a session came to quote vanilla's 10 % slope as live.
  **A function with no `[LIVE-PATCH]` comment is NOT evidence it is unpatched** — check with
  `ghidra_annots.py` (dry run) or `grep -rl "<VA>" build_scripts/` before believing it.

## PM / coder / QA loop

Three subagents in `.claude/agents/` cover both tracks (binary/data modding **and** the Ziggurat
Manual). They are invoked by name — there is no slash command; the main session orchestrates and
decides when to loop.

| agent | does | may edit? |
|---|---|---|
| `aow-pm` | picks the track, finds prior art **and already-recorded failed approaches**, names the hook site / tag / target binary, writes acceptance criteria split into *checkable without the game* vs *in-game only* | no |
| `aow-coder` | writes/edits the `build_*.py` (or `build_ziggurat_manual.py`), **applies** it, reports "applied, untested" | yes |
| `aow-qa` | byte-checks the live binary, reads the cave disassembly, round-trips `--undo`, runs the standing safety checks, reports findings | **no** — reports only |

**The loop:** `aow-pm` → `aow-coder` → `aow-qa` → if FAIL, hand the findings back to `aow-coder`
and re-run QA → on PASS, give the user the in-game checklist. Docs and the master index are
updated **after** the user confirms in-game, per the recording convention above.

Standing decisions baked into these agents:

- **The coder applies patches itself**, killing any running AoW binary to do so (the standing
  authorization above). It does not wait for permission to write to the install.
- **QA never edits.** Findings go back to the coder. This keeps the check independent — QA that
  fixes its own findings stops being a check.
- **Neither can test the game.** Both are told never to write "confirmed working"; every run ends
  with an explicit *needs the user's in-game test* list.

Add a trap to the relevant agent file whenever one costs real time — that is what makes the loop
worth more than three generic roles.

